 DM_FPS	switch	`30` or `60` pins the frame rate the display link and frame pacing aim for; otherwise it follows the game's present interval (60, or 30 when the game asks for 30)
 DM_FPS_HOLD	player	`on` / `off` overrides the profile's `fps_hold`: whether late frames may raise the hold above the frame cap's vsyncs (on by default)
 DM_FPS_TARGET	player	Frame-rate cap: `30` or `60`, `40` for a build whose frame step takes ticks of 1/120 s (`FRAME_STEP_120`: Ocarina of Time; 30 elsewhere, on a screen not at a multiple of 120 Hz, in Low Power Mode, or with `DM_PRESENT_LINK=1`) ([40 fps in ticks of 1/120 s](reference/frame-rate.md#40-fps-in-ticks-of-1120-s)), `120` for a game whose profile has `fps_max 120` (60 otherwise), or `0` for the game's own limit; overrides the profile's `fps_target`
+DM_FRAMEGEN	player	`1` shows, between the real frames of a game on a 120 Hz display link (`DM_PRESENT_LINK=2`) whose presents settle at a steady 2, 3 or 4 ticks apart (60, 40 or 30 fps), blended frames 1/k, 2/k ... (k-1)/k of the way from the previous frame to the current one: 120 presented pictures a second, the game and its frame step untouched; real frames land k-1 ticks (8.3 ms each) later. The cadence is learned from the game's own presents, and a game whose frame times swing is left on stock presents. The first engagement waits out some ten seconds of a settled cadence, so boot and loading keep the stock present; after that it returns within about a second of a settled cadence, pauses at once when the cadence moves (a frame step's 60 to 30 snap) and re-learns, and drops out on a real break, when the game's frame rate falls under generation or when the GPU cannot keep up, waiting longer to return each time it breaks soon after engaging. Off by default
+DM_FRAMEGEN_MAXK	player	`2`, `3` or `4` (default 4): the largest cadence `DM_FRAMEGEN` engages at, that is the lowest real frame rate it multiplies (4: a settled 30 fps becomes about 120 shown). Each step up adds one tick (8.3 ms) before a real frame lands and makes the blends span more motion, so more ghosting on fast movement
+DM_FRAMEGEN_MINK	player	`2` (default), `3` or `4`: the smallest cadence `DM_FRAMEGEN` engages at. `3` leaves a game that holds 60 fps alone (no generation at k=2, where a frame has the least GPU room) and only multiplies 40 and 30 fps
+DM_LATENCY_ACTIVITY	player	On by default: the host holds a latency-critical activity assertion for the life of the process (no App Nap, no timer coalescing), so a controller-only session is not throttled the way a session with pointer events is. `0` leaves it out
 DM_FS	diagnostic	When set, logs file opens, paths not found or not mounted, and a read summary every 64 MiB or 1000 reads
 DM_FSLOG	diagnostic	`=<file>` writes one TSV line per ReadFile: seconds since boot, guest path, offset, length and guest buffer address
 DM_FSLOG_HASH	diagnostic	With `DM_FSLOG`, adds a column with the FNV-1a 64 hash of the bytes read
